<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>PayloadDisplayName</key>
    <string>ContentGuard Background Task Management</string>
    <key>PayloadIdentifier</key>
    <string>com.contentguard.backgroundtaskmanagement</string>
    <key>PayloadType</key>
    <string>Configuration</string>
    <key>PayloadUUID</key>
    <string>1A87F152-84A3-42D9-B4BF-E517529A642C</string>
    <key>PayloadVersion</key>
    <integer>1</integer>
    <key>PayloadScope</key>
    <string>System</string>
    <key>PayloadRemovalDisallowed</key>
    <true/>
    <key>PayloadContent</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>PayloadType</key>
            <string>com.apple.servicemanagement</string>
            <key>PayloadIdentifier</key>
            <string>com.contentguard.backgroundtaskmanagement.payload</string>
            <key>PayloadUUID</key>
            <string>5FCC2714-82D9-47DB-B9AA-5BA453563EA4</string>
            <key>PayloadVersion</key>
            <integer>1</integer>
            <key>PayloadDisplayName</key>
            <string>ContentGuard Background Task Management Payload</string>
            <!--
              Found the hard way, on the real Mac: the "Allow in the
              Background" toggle in System Settings > General > Login
              Items & Extensions is not cosmetic - toggling it off for a
              LaunchDaemon/LaunchAgent actually disables it via
              backgroundtaskmanagementd, no sudo required. Confirmed live
              that com.contentguard.daemon and com.contentguard.agent
              were both freely toggleable there, with no "configured by a
              profile" lock, while other background items (macOS system
              helpers, whatever pushed the generic Background Task
              Management defaults already in place from prior MDM
              enrollment) already showed that badge. This is the payload
              that was missing - without it, a local admin (or, before
              Phase 5's demotion lands, the standard user too) can defeat
              the entire enforcement stack with two clicks in Settings and
              no sudo, no killed process for EscalationManager to notice,
              and no daemon tripwire watching for it at all.

              Two rules, not more - deliberately scoped to exactly the two
              components this project's whole threat model depends on
              staying alive, not every background item on the machine:

              1. Label match on ContentGuard's own two launchd jobs -
                 exact values confirmed against
                 mac/LaunchDaemons/com.contentguard.daemon.plist and
                 mac/LaunchAgents/com.contentguard.agent.plist's own
                 <key>Label</key> entries. Label, not
                 BundleIdentifier/TeamIdentifier, because ContentGuard is
                 self-signed with no real Apple Developer Team ID (see
                 profiles/pppc.mobileconfig's own notes on the
                 self-signed "ContentGuard Signing" identity) - Label is
                 the one rule type that doesn't depend on signing at all.

              2. TeamIdentifier match on Santa's real, public, stable
                 Team ID (ZMCG7MLDV9 - same value already confirmed and
                 used in profiles/system-extension.mobileconfig for
                 Santa's System Extension pre-approval). Deliberately a
                 TeamIdentifier rule rather than naming Santa's individual
                 launchd labels one by one - covers santad, syncd, and any
                 other launch item northpolesec/santa ships or adds in a
                 future version, all under one signed identity, without
                 this profile needing to be updated every time Santa's
                 own packaging changes.

              Once pushed, re-check System Settings > General > Login
              Items & Extensions: both ContentGuard's and Santa's rows
              should show the "This setting has been configured by a
              profile" badge and stop being toggleable, matching how the
              generic system-helper rows already behave in that same
              list.
            -->
            <key>Rules</key>
            <array>
                <dict>
                    <key>RuleType</key>
                    <string>Label</string>
                    <key>RuleValue</key>
                    <string>com.contentguard.daemon</string>
                    <key>Comment</key>
                    <string>ContentGuardDaemon - root-owned tamper anchor, must not be user-disableable</string>
                </dict>
                <dict>
                    <key>RuleType</key>
                    <string>Label</string>
                    <key>RuleValue</key>
                    <string>com.contentguard.agent</string>
                    <key>Comment</key>
                    <string>ContentGuardAgent - screen capture/detection agent, must not be user-disableable</string>
                </dict>
                <dict>
                    <key>RuleType</key>
                    <string>TeamIdentifier</string>
                    <key>RuleValue</key>
                    <string>ZMCG7MLDV9</string>
                    <key>Comment</key>
                    <string>Santa (northpolesec) - EndpointSecurity binary authorization, must not be user-disableable</string>
                </dict>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
